<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Gath</h1><p class="st-meta">1175 BCE – 830 BCE (approx.) · capital Gath</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gath, the largest Philistine city in the 10th and 9th centuries BCE, home of Goliath in the Bible, destroyed by Aram-Damascus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gath, at Tell es-Safi in the Judaean foothills, was a Canaanite town before Philistine settlers arrived in the 12th century BCE. It became one of the five cities of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/philistine-pentapolis">Philistine Pentapolis</a>, guarding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-elah">valley of Elah</a> between the coastal plain and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Judah</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>By the 10th century BCE Gath was the leading Philistine city, covering some 50 hectares. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Bible</a> tells of its king Achish sheltering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and of the giant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goliath">Goliath</a>; an inscription found at the site contains two names resembling Goliath. Its temples show a mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and local traditions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 830 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aram-damascus">Aram-Damascus</a> besieged and destroyed Gath; the siege trench he dug has been excavated. The city never recovered its leading role.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gath.jpg" alt="Gath" width="320" height="170"><figcaption>Image: Bukvoed · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tel-Tsafit-206.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1175 BCE – 830 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gath</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Achish</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Philistine religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Philistine, Canaanite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Philistine Gath, Tell es-Safi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1002&amp;focus=gath" aria-label="Gath on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5578 1024 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gath lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5898 1280L5927 1218"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5898 1280h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5898 1280h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1002&amp;focus=gath">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

